# Shell scripts must be LF in the repository, whatever the checkout does.
#
# install.sh is served raw from GitHub and piped straight into sh. A single
# carriage return at the end of the shebang makes the kernel look for an
# interpreter called "/bin/sh\r", and the whole install dies with
# "bad interpreter: no such file or directory" on the very first line.
*.sh text eol=lf
install.sh text eol=lf

# The same trap, one layer down: these are copied into containers and run by
# a Linux shell.
Dockerfile text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf

# And the mirror image of it. SoundStorm-Setup.cmd is downloaded straight from
# raw.githubusercontent, which serves whatever is in the repository - so the
# repository has to hold CRLF. cmd.exe tolerates LF for simple lines but not
# reliably for `goto`, which this file uses, and the failure is a batch script
# that silently does the wrong half of itself.
# -text, not eol=crlf. Git normalises every *text* file to LF in the
# repository whatever the checkout rule says, and raw.githubusercontent serves
# the repository - so eol=crlf would fix the checkout and change nothing about
# the file people actually download. Marking it not-text stores the bytes
# verbatim, and the bytes are CRLF.
*.cmd -text
*.bat -text

# Media in the starter library and the screenshots are bytes, not text.
*.png binary
*.mp3 binary
*.epub binary
*.pdf binary
